</oai_dc:dc></metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.uniandes.edu.co:1992/79892</identifier><datestamp>2026-08-29T09:00:25Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1992_52068</setSpec><setSpec>com_1992_51805</setSpec><setSpec>com_1992_52187</setSpec><setSpec>com_1992_51844</setSpec><setSpec>col_1992_52087</setSpec><setSpec>col_1992_52277</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>Uncovering heterogeneity: Skills allocation and mismatches in the Colombian labour market</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Díaz Gamboa, Sebastián Andrés</dc:creator>
   <dc:contributor>Ham González, Andrés</dc:contributor>
   <dc:contributor>Fernández Sierra, Manuel</dc:contributor>
   <dc:contributor>Maldonado Carrizosa, Dario</dc:contributor>
   <dc:contributor>Becerra Camargo, Oscar Reinaldo</dc:contributor>
   <dc:subject>Skill mismatch</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Unemployment</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Specialization of labour</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Education</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Vocational selection</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Semantic</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Economía</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Gobierno y Asuntos Públicos</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>In this article, I develop an empirical approach to measure skill mismatch and examine its relationship with unemployment in Colombia. I propose a metric to assess the correspondence between fields of specialization and occupations (the horizontal dimension), exploiting textual descriptions of jobs and educational programs. This approach provides a potential alternative to existing measurement strategies by reducing subjectivity and strengthening objective matching criteria. Following this, using a Logit model, I estimate the incidence of mismatch and its association with unemployment for the second half of 2024. The results indicate that horizontal educational mismatch reaches 43.46% among employed individuals and 40.22% among the unemployed. Moreover, I find that while the horizontal dimension is a significant predictor of unemployment, its effect is smaller than that of vertical mismatch.</dc:description>
   <dc:description>Maestría</dc:description>
   <dc:date>2025-05-26</dc:date>
