</oai_dc:dc></metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.uniandes.edu.co:1992/77803</identifier><datestamp>2026-01-23T09:00:24Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1992_52318</setSpec><setSpec>com_1992_52212</setSpec><setSpec>com_1992_51811</setSpec><setSpec>col_1992_52363</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>Towards symmetric crystal structure prediction with flow matching equivariant transformers</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>González Estrada, Gabriel Francisco</dc:creator>
   <dc:contributor>Arbeláez Escalante, Pablo Andrés</dc:contributor>
   <dc:contributor>Giraldo Trujillo, Luis Felipe</dc:contributor>
   <dc:contributor>Macías López, Mario Alberto</dc:contributor>
   <dc:contributor>Biomedical Computer Vision (BCV), Center for Research and Formation in Artificial Intelligence (CinfonIA)</dc:contributor>
   <dc:subject>Crystal Structure Prediction</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Flow Matching</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Classifier-Free Guidance</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Equivariance</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Transformers</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Symmetry</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Space Groups</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Ingeniería</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>At the intersection of materials science and 3D vision, crystals appear as structured atomic point clouds that encode symmetry and periodic repetition, aligning naturally with geometric reasoning. These structures drive advances in materials discovery, pharmaceutical development, and modern technology. Predicting a correct arrangement remains challenging because crystals must satisfy symmetry and periodicity over a large combinatorial space. While recent generative frameworks address this task, they still rely on weak evaluation metrics and representations that overlook crystalline geometric constraints. To overcome these challenges, we cast Crystal Structure Prediction as a 3D vision problem by modeling crystals as symmetry-constrained point clouds. We introduce the Graph Flow Matching Equivariant Transformer (GraFMET), an SE(3)-Equivariant generative model, and apply classifier-free guidance to the crystal domain using the space group as a condition. Finally, we introduce a polymorph-aware protocol that adapts point cloud metrics to crystals. Across four standard datasets, GraFMET achieves a new state-of-the-art, achieving significant improvements in 3D distance metrics and demonstrating that 3D vision and image generation techniques can be applied effectively to scientific applications, such as materials discovery.</dc:description>
   <dc:description>Maestría</dc:description>
   <dc:description>Generative AI</dc:description>
   <dc:description>Geometric Deep Learning</dc:description>
   <dc:description>Crystal Structure Prediction</dc:description>
   <dc:date>2026-01-19</dc:date>
   <dc:type>Trabajo de grado - Maestría</dc:type>
   <dc:type>info:eu-repo/semantics/masterThesis</dc:type>
   <dc:type>info:eu-repo/semantics/acceptedVersion</dc:type>
   <dc:type>http://purl.org/coar/resource_type/c_bdcc</dc:type>
   <dc:type>http://purl.org/coar/version/c_ab4af688f83e57aa</dc:type>
   <dc:type>Text</dc:type>
