</oai_dc:dc></metadata></record><record><header><identifier>oai:repositorio.uniandes.edu.co:1992/73425</identifier><datestamp>2025-12-12T20:46:06Z</datestamp><setSpec>com_1992_52323</setSpec><setSpec>com_1992_52211</setSpec><setSpec>com_1992_51811</setSpec><setSpec>col_1992_52359</setSpec></header><metadata><oai_dc:dc xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:doc="http://www.lyncode.com/xoai" xmlns:oai_dc="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc/ http://www.openarchives.org/OAI/2.0/oai_dc.xsd">
   <dc:title>Data-based probabilistic model for the evaluation of the state of resilience of the Colombian main road network</dc:title>
   <dc:creator>Villamil Martínez, William David</dc:creator>
   <dc:contributor>Caro Spinel, Silvia</dc:contributor>
   <dc:contributor>Sánchez Silva, Edgar Mauricio</dc:contributor>
   <dc:contributor>Caicedo Hormaza, Bernardo</dc:contributor>
   <dc:contributor>Estrada Mejía, Nicolás</dc:contributor>
   <dc:contributor>Facultad de Ingeniería::Geomateriales y Sistemas de Infraestructura</dc:contributor>
   <dc:subject>Resilience</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Road infrastructure</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Colombian main road network</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Disruptive events</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Climate change</dc:subject>
   <dc:subject>Ingeniería</dc:subject>
   <dc:description>One of the consequences of climate change has been the increase in the intensity and frequency of phenomena and events that can disrupt the functioning of different infrastructures, including road infrastructure. Due to this, governmental and non-governmental institutions have made various efforts to develop strategies and plans for promoting and implementing resilience in Colombian road infrastructure. However, these efforts have focused mainly on launching action plans and disaster risk management, which are not based on a diagnostic study of the resilience of Colombian road infrastructure. In this context, this work aims to develop a probabilistic model based on data from disruptive events to evaluate the state of resilience of the Colombian main road network. For this, the construction and processing of a database of events that have caused disruptions in the national road network between 2017 and 2023 was carried out. Based on this database, the calibration of each of the parameters of the probabilistic model was done, through which random disruptive events were generated in the network over a time horizon of 50 years, and the recovery time from these events of each of the sections of the Colombian road network was estimated. From the above, the losses were calculated in terms of traffic that stops circulating due to road closures in different scenarios of alteration of the frequencies of occurrence of events. Additionally, the importance of each of the sections in the operation of the road network was estimated. Through this model, an indicator was defined that characterizes the resilience of each of the sections of the main Colombian road network, integrating its average recovery time from disruptive events, its importance in the network's functioning, and its traffic volumes. The results show that over time, the losses in the network increase, also showing a high sensitivity to the alteration of the frequencies of occurrence of events. On the other hand, through the defined resilience indicator, the most critical sections of the network were identified. The three most critical sections are Sogamoso – El Crucero, Duitama – La Palmera, and Asermanuevo – La Virginia. For this reason, they should be a priority when making decisions and allocating resources to promote and improve the resilience of the Colombian main road network.</dc:description>
   <dc:description>Magíster en Ingeniería Civil</dc:description>
   <dc:description>Maestría</dc:description>
   <dc:description>Infraestructura Vial</dc:description>
   <dc:description>Sistemas de Infraestructura</dc:description>
   <dc:date>2023-12-12</dc:date>
